  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and well above normal conditions will persist \nthrough Sunday.\n\nSurface high pressure centered over the Mid-Atlantic will shift \nacross the Northeast/New England today and the first half of Sunday \ncausing winds to become more southerly, limiting any lake \ninfluences. Downsloping flow in combination with 850mb temperatures \npeaking above the climatological 90th percentile for mid-November, \nwill support high temperatures Saturday afternoon a good 15 to 20 \ndegrees above average. The warm weather will then persist  through \nSunday ahead of the upper level pattern shift early next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler air arrives next week, supporting chances for \nsome light rain and/or snow early in the week.\n\nThe mid to upper level pattern late this weekend and into the \nearliest portions of next week will feature a trough overspreading \nthe region from the north-central United States Sunday to the \neastern Great Lakes through Tuesday, where a ridge will then build \neast Tuesday night through Wednesday. Meanwhile at the surface, a \ncold front will sweep east across western and north-central New York \nSunday night, with surface high pressure gradually shifting east \nacross north-central United States and into the Great Lakes Monday \nthrough Wednesday.\n\nGetting more into the specifics, with the frontal passage Sunday \nnight into Monday morning, expect scattered rain showers to \noverspread the region from west to east due to low-level convergence \nand moist ascent along the cold front and moist isentropic lift \nahead of the trough axis aloft. Overall, precipitation amounts will \nbe very light.\n\nIn the wake of the frontal passage, a colder airmass will filter \nacross the region Monday. While a dry weather is expected to return, \nthe post-front airmass should be cold and moist enough to support \nlake-effect rain and/or snow showers southeast and south of the \neastern Great Lakes Monday into Tuesday. Any lake-effect \nprecipitation should end by Wednesday as a lowering subsidence \ninversion and continued low-level dry air advection cause lake-\ninduced instability to wane.",
